 "caveats": [
  "NASA POWER cells are about 55 by 48 km. Each cell's temperature is an average over canyons and uplands, while the grain grows mostly on the uplands, so heat comparisons between areas are rougher than rain comparisons.",
  "Crop shares come from USDA crop maps, which can mislabel some fields.",
  "16 dryland cells is a small sample; the rain relationship is strong, the exact cutoff is rough.",
  "This field's own grid cell is not one of the 16 areas in the regional comparison: dryland grain is 79.7% of its mapped crop acres, under the 80% cut-off that screens out irrigated areas. Its crop shares are still shown as this field's area, but they are not a clean dryland picture.",
  "This field's targets lie outside the range of the dryland areas compared: the driest of them averages 115.8 mm of spring rain and the hottest 36.92 C summer peak heat. The 2045 trend target is 107.5 mm and 38.5 C. The bad-years-become-normal target is 71.6 mm and 38.9 C. The areas listed under twins are the nearest ones, not true matches.",
  "The pin is 2.7 km from the east edge of its NASA POWER cell, and NASA POWER gives the whole cell one value. The next cell east (Ritzville town and east, WA) averages 142.3 mm of spring rain and 36.33 C summer peak heat, against 107.5 mm and 36.88 C in this field's cell. Both are grid estimates, not rain gauge readings. The nearest rain gauge with NOAA 1991 to 2020 normals is RITZVILLE 1 SSE (USC00457059), 9.3 km from the pin and in the next NASA POWER cell (Ritzville town and east, WA), not this field's. Over those same years NASA POWER for this field's cell gives 108.8 mm of March to June rain against 111.0 mm at the gauge (-2%), and 329 mm a year against 324 mm (+2%). NASA POWER for the cell that holds the gauge gives 142.3 mm and 414 mm (+28% and +28% against the gauge; these are 2004 to 2025 averages, the only years in the grid file). So NASA POWER reads about the same as the gauge in this field's cell, and wetter than the gauge in the gauge's own cell.",
  "Peak heat slopes upward 0.49 C per decade on a straight-line fit (p = 0.04), but that is not a clear trend. A rank test, which one extreme year sways less, gives p = 0.08 for all 45 years, and the fit leans on one year, 2021: without it the slope is about 0.35 C per decade (p = 0.11). The average daily high in June and July rose 0.62 C per decade, and that rise is clear on both tests (p = 0.001 and p = 0.001).",
  "Counted on April to June instead, the record shows no clear trend: rain (p = 0.95), days over 32 C (p = 0.68), heat above 32 C (p = 0.26). Hot or dry springs by decade since 1981: 5 of 10, 2 of 10, 8 of 10, 4 of 10, 3 of 5. April to June hot days and peak heat in June and July are different measures, so the two readings do not conflict."
 ],
